## Summary (sess75)

I made decisive diagnostic progress on the sole remaining `cache_coherency` blocker (Face B, shared-dir lost-update), following RULE 4 (instrument → prove, don't guess-patch).

**What I did:**
1. Added always-on `P-DIR-SEQ` lock-event tracing (FASTEX / EX-ACQ-SLOW / PR-ACQ-SLOW / REL for dir inodes) — build `D3AA5D30`, deployed to all 4 nodes.
2. Wrote `tests/faceb_trace.sh` — a focused reproducer that dumps each node's per-inode dir-DLM event counts + cached view synchronized with the failure (reproduces on iter 1).

**Key results (these change the picture materially):**
- **Refuted sess74's core conclusion.** sess74 said "one node monopolizes dir EX forever, never releases." That was a single-run artifact. With real concurrency, the DLM lock protocol **cycles correctly across all 4 nodes** (symmetric ~38 fast-path + ~3 slow-path EX acquires + 3 releases each). Not a deferred-BAST/monopoly bug — don't chase the fast-path-re-grant theory.
- **Nodes genuinely diverge** in cached dir view (e.g. test1 sees n1=40/n2=9; test2 sees n1=32/n2=40) → real on-disk lost update + stale cached reads. The divergent nodes are exactly the ones with `DIR-STALE-SKIP>0`.
- **Localized the stale-unrefreshable block to the dir2 LEAF / hash-index block** (`blk=8388608`), not a data block. It stays stale because every refresh path (acquire drain-evict, release evict, read-hook) must skip it while it's transiently pinned by the node's own CIL-unpin tail. A stale leaf misses a peer's later-added name hashes → lookups miss exactly those names (matches the observed contiguous-suffix loss).

**Status:** cache_coherency still FAIL, rsync_paired still FAIL (PASS=10/12). Criteria **not met** — marker not written. Full sharpened diagnosis + next-step plan (write-side leaf-clobber detector to prove direction; then a leaf-specific drain/refresh fix or a RULE-5 Gemini consult with the much sharper diagnosis) recorded at the top of `state.md`.

